 "memory": {
  "method": "A separate pass after the timing pass. Each (arm, N) runs in a fresh process: imports, the CUDA context created by one tiny device operation, then the baseline read, before the arm builds or allocates anything; then the arm's kernels built and a warm-up run on a 64-sample batch (every compile happens here; JAX also compiles for shape N), so every library's pool reserved during the warm-up counts in the reading; CuPy's pools and PyTorch's cache trimmed after the warm-up; then the arm built for N (eagle's graph, PyTorch's captured graph) and one full run with upload and download, the device read every 2 ms throughout (the masked arms hand their temporaries back after each run, so a reading after it would miss them). Device: the most memory NVML accounted to the process on the timed GPU during the build and the run, minus the baseline. Caveats: each allocator's rounding and growth policy count (JAX's allocator grows in regions that can exceed the request; PyTorch rounds blocks up and a captured CUDA graph keeps a private pool; Warp's stream-ordered pool reserves in chunks of tens of MiB, its release threshold set to 0 after the warm-up); the driver accounts in pages of about 2 MiB, so small-N rows read 0 or one page. Host: the peak resident set (VmHWM, reset to the current RSS at the host baseline, taken after the warm-up) minus the baseline RSS. Minimum: the planes the workload needs, N x (8 float64 planes (state, time, step) x 8 bytes + 2 counters x 4 bytes) = N x 72 bytes.",
